            <history xml:id="history_aa3e62ab">
               <p xml:id="idm75">
                  <rend xml:id="idm76" fontstyle="italic">Grundlage einer Ehren-Pforte</rend> (1740), edited by <persName xml:id="idm77" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Johann Mattheson</persName>, includes an autobiography submitted by <persName xml:id="idm78" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Lorenz Mizler</persName> – a contemporary of Scheibe's who was a physician and mathematician but had also studied music by reading scores and books as well as hearing performances. It is likely that Scheibe knew Mizler from their days in Leipzig where Mizler studied theology at the university and was apparently a good friend of J.S. Bach's. When Mattheson received the autobiography, he decided to edit Mizler's text by adding numerous detailed notes. Using the pseudonym '<persName xml:id="idm79" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Alfonso</persName>' which he already had done in his <rend xml:id="idm80" fontstyle="italic">Der Critische Musicus</rend> (SchW C1:008), Scheibe contributed with a rather harsh critique of Lorenz Mizler's writings on music and published compositions which Mattheson included as an appendix in <rend xml:id="idm81" fontstyle="italic">Grundlage einer Ehren-Pforte</rend>. The criticism might be due to Mizler's rather conservative writings arguing, in agreement with <persName xml:id="idm82" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</persName>, that music belonged to the mathematical disciplines – a view which indeed is evident in his <rend xml:id="idm83" fontstyle="italic">Anfangs-Gründe des General-Basses nach mathematischer Lehr-Art abgehandelt</rend> of 1739. Mattheson and Scheibe as well as <persName xml:id="idm84" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName> (e.g. his <rend xml:id="idm85" fontstyle="italic">Vier und zwanzig theils ernsthafte, theils scherzende, Oden</rend> of 1741, actually dedicated to Scheibe) opposed the idea of composing music according to mathematical rules. Since the first appearance of <rend xml:id="idm86" fontstyle="italic">Der Critische Musicus</rend> in 1738, Mizler had published long reviews of Scheibe's discussions in his own periodical <rend xml:id="idm87" fontstyle="italic">Neu eröffnete musikalische Bibliothek</rend>, a fact that might have induced further criticism on Scheibe's part.</p>
            </history>
